oken he  has that in common with angela merkel  well charles wants to use its presidency  of the the g7 to develop it into a  climate club what what's his  plan yeah well this is the the prime  example we can expect to hear this  expression uh a climate club uh quite a  bit this year now it's an idea that's  been going around in academic circles  for a few years and it's trying to  tackle this problem that we've had with  climate change treaties over the years  that there aren't enough incentives for  countries to really follow through on  the promises  that they make and at the heart of this  is cost if you want to cut climate  change if you want to fight climate  change you need to raise the price of  polluting carbon and countries worry  that if they do that in their country  then companies might just up sticks to  other countries especially big  industries and  move their operations there where they  don't pay such a high price for carbon  so the idea with a carbon climate club  is that you get a group of like-minded  countries that want to press ahead  that set roughly equivalent standards on  the pricing of carbon on the pricing of  pollution and other things and they  agree to trade under favorable terms  with each other and crucially countries  that are outside it don't get those  favorable terms so that's an incentive  for those countries to join so you can  expect olaf schultz to be bringing this  up at the g7 this year and trying to use  it as a springboard for this idea  so uh how likely is this to be  successful then how big is germany's and  olaf schultz influence in this club of  rich countries  well i think  he certainly has potential as a  mobilizer to get something like this  going but the big question about the  climate club basing it around the g7  would be well is it enough if you don't  have really big economies like china  involved and you already have industry  associations in germany saying exactly  that if china's not involved in  something